  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nA read-backed HTTP POST using `CURLOPT_RESUME_FROM_LARGE` with a non-seekable source silently uploads the wrong data when the server returns a 307 or 308 redirect before the request body is sent. `curl_easy_perform()` returns `CURLE_OK`, so the caller cannot detect the substitution.\n\nThe bug is in `cr_in_resume_from()` (`lib/sendf.c`): when it falls back to reading-and-discarding through `ctx->read_cb` (no seek callback available), it does not set `ctx->has_used_cb`. On redirect, `cr_in_needs_rewind()` checks only that flag and reports no rewind needed. A fresh reader is created and `http_resume()` applies the persistent `resume_from` offset a second time. For a source of configured size N and offset O, the followed request sends N−O bytes starting at source offset 2O instead of O.\n\nIntroduced by commit `14bcea074a` (\"lib: enhance client reader resume + rewind\"), present since curl 8.7.0. Tested against commit `c2676bf9e639`.\n\n## Root cause detail\n\n1. `cr_in_resume_from()` at `lib/sendf.c:798-820` invokes `ctx->read_cb` to skip past the offset but never sets `ctx->has_used_cb = TRUE`. The normal read path at line 687 does set it.\n\n2. `cr_in_needs_rewind()` at `lib/sendf.c:755-760` returns only `ctx->has_used_cb`, so `http_perhapsrewind()` concludes no rewind is needed.\n\n3. `http_resume()` at `lib/http.c:2400` guards with `!data->req.authneg` and a comment saying \"only act on the first request,\" but that condition does not exclude redirects. On the followed 308, `set_reader()` + `http_resume()` runs again (lines 3112-3116) and applies the offset a second time.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nDocker and a curl source tree at commit `c2676bf9e639` or later (the bug is still present on HEAD).\n\n### Steps\n\n1. Build curl from source at commit `c2676bf9e639` (or any commit since 8.7.0):\n\n```sh\ncmake -S . -B build \\\n  -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON -DBUILD_CURL_EXE=OFF -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF \\\n  -DCURL_USE_OPENSSL=OFF -DCURL_USE_LIBPSL=OFF -DCURL_ZLIB=OFF\ncmake --build build -j$(nproc)\n```\n\n2. Compile the client:\n\n```sh\ncc client.c -Iinclude -Lbuild/lib -lcurl -o poc-client\n```\n\n3. Start the server:\n\n```sh\npython3 -u server.py &\n```\n\n4. Run the client:\n\n```sh\nLD_LIBRARY_PATH=build/lib ./poc-client\n```\n\n### Expected output (fixed curl)\n\nThe sink receives 4096 bytes of `B` (byte value 66), `curl_easy_perform` returns 0, and the callback consumes 8192 bytes total (4096 skipped + 4096 sent).\n\n### Actual output (vulnerable curl)\n\n```\nSINK_LENGTH=4096\nSINK_BYTE_VALUES=[67]\nVERDICT=VULNERABLE\nCURL_RESULT=0\nSOURCE_BYTES_CONSUMED=12288\n```\n\nThe sink receives 4096 bytes of `C` (byte value 67). The callback consumed 12288 bytes: the offset was applied twice (4096 skipped on first request + 4096 skipped again on redirected request + 4096 `C` bytes sent). `curl_easy_perform` returns `CURLE_OK`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nSet `ctx->has_used_cb = TRUE` in `cr_in_resume_from()` when the fallback read-and-discard path invokes `ctx->read_cb`. This makes `cr_in_needs_rewind()` return true after resume, so redirect handling requests a rewind. Seekable sources rewind and reapply the offset once; non-seekable sources fail with `CURLE_SEND_FAIL_REWIND` instead of silently uploading the wrong interval.\n\nI am aware that `docs/cmdline-opts/continue-at.md` describes HTTP upload resume as non-standard and \"not guaranteed.\" The issue here is the local reader-position bookkeeping and the silent wrong-body success, not server-side interoperability.\n\n## Impact\n\nA malicious or misconfigured server returning an early body-preserving redirect causes libcurl to upload bytes from a different region of the application's upload source while reporting success. The caller has no indication that the wrong data was sent.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- Requires a read-backed POST/PUT with a positive resume offset.\n- The source must lack a seek callback (non-seekable).\n- `CURLOPT_FOLLOWLOCATION` must be enabled (off by default in libcurl).\n- The server must return 307/308 before the body is sent.\n- The read callback must be able to supply data beyond the configured upload size.\n\nDoes not read arbitrary process memory; the extra bytes come from the application's own read callback.",
